1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:03,170
This is Swift Sword.

2
00:00:03,170 --> 00:00:06,945
A 3D samurai fighting game that runs in a browser tab.

3
00:00:06,945 --> 00:00:10,070
Its code was written entirely by an AI.

4
00:00:10,070 --> 00:00:13,510
But the game you're looking at was not the AI's idea.

5
00:00:13,510 --> 00:00:18,160
This is the story of how it got here, and what actually made it good.

6
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:24,410
It began on June 11th, with a single sentence.

7
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:33,905
Minutes later, Claude Fable 5 had a playable game.

8
00:00:33,905 --> 00:00:38,390
Two fighters drawn in code, punches, kicks, a health bar.

9
00:00:38,390 --> 00:00:41,560
It worked. It also looked like this.

10
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:46,100
So the directing began. Don't copy the art.

11
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:49,165
Invent your own warriors. Make them bigger.

12
00:00:49,165 --> 00:00:52,450
Then came a pencil sketch. This is my art.

13
00:00:52,450 --> 00:00:54,160
Use characters like these.

14
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:57,000
Each note took the model minutes to build.

15
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,025
And each note was a person, looking at the screen, and saying:

16
00:01:01,025 --> 00:01:02,090
not yet.

17
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,195
On day two came the question that would define the whole project.

18
00:01:07,195 --> 00:01:11,266
Why aren't we using WebGL, and other advanced tech,

19
00:01:11,266 --> 00:01:13,560
for more appealing characters?

20
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:15,745
In June, the honest answer was pixel art.

21
00:01:15,745 --> 00:01:18,610
Real sprite fighters replaced the drawings.

22
00:01:18,610 --> 00:01:21,120
And the reply was four words.

23
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:23,160
Now we are talking.

24
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:28,375
From there, the model wrote the code, and taste did the steering.

25
00:01:28,375 --> 00:01:31,690
The sword looks longer than its hit. Fix it.

26
00:01:31,690 --> 00:01:35,170
The sound is synthetic. Find real recordings.

27
00:01:35,170 --> 00:01:38,010
The music should be grim, and Japanese.

28
00:01:38,010 --> 00:01:41,610
Add blood. Slow it down, it feels rushed.

29
00:01:41,610 --> 00:01:44,625
Add rain, lightning, falling leaves.

30
00:01:44,625 --> 00:01:45,125
More drama.

31
00:01:45,125 --> 00:01:48,810
None of these are engineering instructions.

32
00:01:48,810 --> 00:01:50,700
They are a director's notes.

33
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:53,955
It got a name: Swift Sword.

34
00:01:53,955 --> 00:01:55,570
It got touch controls,

35
00:01:55,570 --> 00:01:59,215
and an iPhone audio bug that took two rounds to fix.

36
00:01:59,215 --> 00:02:01,440
Then the bar was raised.

37
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:04,365
No half efforts anymore. A full plan.

38
00:02:04,365 --> 00:02:07,925
Four fighters, three stages, an arcade ladder.

39
00:02:07,925 --> 00:02:09,780
Built that same night.

40
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:16,055
A few days later, the work moved to a different model, Claude Opus 4.8,

41
00:02:16,055 --> 00:02:17,445
and a new question.

42
00:02:17,445 --> 00:02:19,845
What engine could lift this further?

43
00:02:19,845 --> 00:02:24,490
The answer was PixiJS, and a GPU effects layer.

44
00:02:24,490 --> 00:02:26,365
Bloom, particles, shockwaves.

45
00:02:26,365 --> 00:02:30,375
Five days and about thirty messages in,

46
00:02:30,375 --> 00:02:32,880
this was the ceiling of the 2D game.

47
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,900
Then, nothing. For nearly five months.

48
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:42,345
On November 10th, with a new model, Claude Fable 5.2, came one message.

49
00:02:42,345 --> 00:02:47,000
This is dated. I need a complete revamp.

50
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,290
Nothing but the highest effort.

51
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,565
This time, there was no back and forth.

52
00:02:53,565 --> 00:02:57,540
From that one prompt, the model designed three fighters.

53
00:02:57,540 --> 00:03:00,750
Using generative tools, it created concept art,

54
00:03:00,750 --> 00:03:02,580
turned it into 3D meshes, rigged them,

55
00:03:02,580 --> 00:03:07,620
and retargeted a library of sword animations onto each skeleton.

56
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:11,280
When the generation credits ran out before the third fighter was rigged,

57
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:13,650
it wrote its own auto-rigging tool.

58
00:03:13,650 --> 00:03:18,055
It generated three arenas, built the lighting, the weather, the camera,

59
00:03:18,055 --> 00:03:21,375
and a fighting engine with its own tests.

60
00:03:21,375 --> 00:03:22,355
And it shipped.

61
00:03:22,355 --> 00:03:25,750
One hour and forty-three minutes after the prompt.

62
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:28,375
It still wasn't finished.

63
00:03:28,375 --> 00:03:31,685
On a real graphics card, the screen went black.

64
00:03:31,685 --> 00:03:35,185
Something the model's own test machine could never show it.

65
00:03:35,185 --> 00:03:38,875
One screenshot from a human was enough to find the cause.

66
00:03:38,875 --> 00:03:41,160
Minutes later, it was fixed.

67
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:44,940
So what changed between June and November?

68
00:03:44,940 --> 00:03:46,305
The model did.

69
00:03:46,305 --> 00:03:49,840
The question from day two, why can't this look better,

70
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:52,980
went from pixel art, to real-time 3D.

71
00:03:52,980 --> 00:03:54,605
And from thirty messages, to one.

72
00:03:54,605 --> 00:03:58,755
What did not change, is where the game came from.

73
00:03:58,755 --> 00:04:00,855
Grim. Japanese. Cinematic. More drama.

74
00:04:00,855 --> 00:04:04,387
Every one of those words came from a person,

75
00:04:04,387 --> 00:04:08,600
months before the technology could deliver them.

76
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:11,220
The models keep getting better at building.

77
00:04:11,220 --> 00:04:13,115
Knowing what is worth building?

78
00:04:13,115 --> 00:04:15,510
That is still the human part.

79
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,995
Swift Sword. Imagined by a person.

80
00:04:19,995 --> 00:04:21,645
Built with Claude.

81
00:04:21,645 --> 00:04:23,950
Play it now, in your browser.
